body:
`nginx/init-letsencrypt.sh` aborts during the TLS bootstrap with a "syntax errors" message, but `nginx.conf` is valid. The actual failure is `[emerg] host not found in upstream "app:3000"`: the script starts nginx with `--no-deps`, while the production config declares `upstream app { server app:3000; }`, which open-source nginx resolves at startup — and `app` isn't running yet. The script's `nginx -t >/dev/null 2>&1` discards the real reason.

### Changes

- **`nginx/nginx.bootstrap.conf` (new)** — minimal port-80-only config serving just the ACME webroot. No `upstream{}`, no TLS server, so it boots without `app` and without certs.
- **`nginx/init-letsencrypt.sh`**
  - Swaps `nginx.conf` ↔ `nginx.bootstrap.conf` under an `EXIT` trap that always restores the original (even on abort/Ctrl-C).
  - `up -d --force-recreate` so a leftover container from a previous failed run picks up the swapped config.
  - On `nginx -t` failure, prints the actual `nginx -t` output and the last 50 nginx log lines before exiting — so the next person sees the real cause on first read.
  - After issuance, restores `nginx.conf` and stops the bootstrap nginx so the operator's `docker compose up -d` brings it up alongside `app` (no resolver flapping window).
- **`.gitignore`** — ignores the transient `nginx/.nginx.conf.bootstrap-backup` in case the script is killed mid-swap.
- **`docs/tls-setup.md`** — documents the bootstrap file and the common failure modes the new error output now surfaces.

### Bootstrap config (excerpt)

```nginx
server {
    listen 80 default_server;
    server_name _;

    location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ {
        root /var/www/certbot;
    }
    location / { return 404; }
}
```

### Notes for reviewers

- `docker compose down nginx` is intentionally **not** used to tear down the bootstrap container — `down` ignores the service argument and would take the whole stack with it. `stop` + `rm -f` is per-service.
- The production `nginx.conf` is unchanged; the upstream-at-startup behavior is a feature of OSS nginx (NGINX Plus has dynamic resolution), and switching to runtime `resolver` + variables would cost the `keepalive` upstream pool. Isolating the workaround to the bootstrap path keeps the hot path untouched.
